<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Ballad · Lament · 1913 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Danny Boy</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A song of farewell written by an English lawyer in 1910 and sung to an old Irish tune, the Londonderry Air.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Frederic Weatherly</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>traditional (the Irish air &quot;Londonderry Air&quot;)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>English songwriter's words to an Irish air; sung across the English-speaking world</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Bath, Somerset, England (the words); the air from Limavady, County Londonderry, present-day Northern Ireland</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>words 1910; set to &quot;Londonderry Air&quot; in 1913</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Ballad · Lament</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words by Frederic Weatherly (1910, set to the air in 1913; he died in 1929))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>The words of &quot;Danny Boy&quot; were written in 1910 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bath">Bath, Somerset</a>, by the English lawyer and lyricist Frederic Weatherly, first to a tune other than the one now sung. The melody, &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derry">Londonderry</a> Air&quot;, is a traditional Irish air; Jane Ross of Limavady is credited with collecting it in the mid-19th century from a musician she met. How words and air came together is told in more than one way. In one account Weatherly's sister-in-law Margaret Enright Weatherly, known as Jess, sent him a copy of the air in 1913 and he reworked his words to fit its rhyme and metre. In another she sang the air to him in 1912 with different words. In a third, Weatherly never set the poem to any tune himself: Jess, who lived with her husband Edward Weatherly at the Neosho mine near Ouray, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colorado">Colorado</a>, set it to the air in 1913, having heard it as a child in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a>, played by her father and other Irish railway workers.</p><p>Weatherly gave the song to the singer Elsie Griffin, who made it one of the most popular songs of the new century, and Ernestine Schumann-Heink made the first recording. Sheet music of 1918 bearing Weatherly's printed signature offered alternative words for a male singer, under the title &quot;Eily Dear&quot;, so that &quot;Danny Boy&quot; itself would be sung only by a woman; whether this was Weatherly's intent is unclear, and in practice men and women sing the same words.</p><p>The meaning of the song has been much debated; one common reading takes it as a parent's message to a son going off to war. The tune travelled on its own as well: Percy Grainger arranged the air for wind ensemble as &quot;Irish Tune from County <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derry">Derry</a>&quot; in 1918.</p><p>Through the 20th century the song was recorded many times. Judy Garland sang it in the film Little Nellie Kelly (1940) and on stage in Ireland, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>; Glenn Miller, Bing Crosby, Al Hibbler, Conway Twitty, Andy Williams, Jackie Wilson, Elvis Presley and Johnny Cash all recorded it. In 1957 Harry Belafonte opened it with a spoken introduction set in an Irish rising and changed its words to make it more plainly a song against war, and in 1993 Sinéad O'Connor sang it with an added half verse about dying for Ireland.</p><p>The song is often sung at funerals, though the National Catholic Reporter wrote in 2001 that it cannot be played during Mass.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A song of parting, in which someone who stays behind says goodbye to a young man who is leaving and hopes for his return. It is full of longing and the thought that the one left behind may not live to see him come home. Many listeners have heard in it a parent sending a son to war.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">The words</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Oh, Danny Boy, the pipes, the pipes are calling</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>From glen to glen, and down the mountain side,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>The summer's gone, and all the roses falling,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>It's you, it's you must go and I must bide.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>But come ye back when summer's in the meadow,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Or when the valley's hushed and white with snow,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>It's I'll be here in sunshine or in shadow,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Oh, Danny Boy, oh Danny Boy, I love you so!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>But when ye come, and all the flowers are dying,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>If I am dead, as dead I well may be,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Ye'll come and find the place where I am lying,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And kneel and say an Ave there for me;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And I shall hear, though soft you tread above me,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And all my grave will warmer, sweeter be,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>For you will bend and tell me that you love me,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And I shall sleep in peace until you come to me!</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Words: Frederic Weatherly (1913). Text from <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Danny_Boy" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p></div></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="listen"><h2>Listen</h2><div class="sg-video" data-yt="ebulR9HNvY8" data-title="Danny Boy: Emmet Cahill, tenor"><button type="button" class="sg-play"><span class="sg-play-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="sg-play-t">Play the performance</span><span class="sg-play-n">Emmet Cahill, tenor</span></button></div><p class="sg-video-cr">On YouTube, from the channel Emmet Cahill Irish Tenor. Pressing play loads YouTube&#8217;s player. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ebulR9HNvY8" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch on YouTube<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a></p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Irish Tune from County Derry (Percy Grainger)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1918</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same melody, arranged for wind ensemble</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Eily Dear</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1918</span><span class="sg-chain-h">alternative words for a male singer, printed with the sheet music</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Little Nellie Kelly, film (sung by Judy Garland)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1940</span><span class="sg-chain-h">used in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Glenn Miller and His Orchestra</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1940</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recording</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Bing Crosby, single with &quot;I'll Be Home for Christmas&quot;</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1941</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recording</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">An Evening with Harry Belafonte</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1957</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recording with a spoken introduction and changed words</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Conway Twitty</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1959</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recording (No. 10 on the Billboard Hot 100)</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">One After 909 (the Beatles, Let It Be)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1970</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quoted in (John Lennon sings a snippet at the end)</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">From Elvis Presley Boulevard, Memphis, Tennessee (Elvis Presley)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1976</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recording</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Sinéad O'Connor on The Late Late Show</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1993</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recording with an added half verse</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">American IV: The Man Comes Around (Johnny Cash)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2002</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recording</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Londonderry Air</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">mid-19th century (collected by Jane Ross of Limavady)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same melody</span></li></ul></section>
